<div class="mb-12" data-reveal>
  <h2 class="mb-5 text-[26px] font-semibold leading-[1.2] tracking-tight text-[#111827] md:text-[30px]">Week 1: define and measure</h2>
  <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    <li>Write a one-page scope: what the agent does, what it never does, and when it hands over to a person.</li>
    <li>Define “done” in a sentence with one example that counts and one that does not. Vague outcomes are the hardest part of agent projects, as our buying guidance on outcome-based pricing notes.</li>
    <li>Pull your baseline from the last month of real work.</li>
    <li>Set thresholds now, not after you see results. For example, the lowest accuracy you would accept and the highest review time per item. The numbers are yours to choose.</li>
    <li>Tell the affected staff what is being tested and why.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<div class="mb-12" data-reveal>
  <h2 class="mb-5 text-[26px] font-semibold leading-[1.2] tracking-tight text-[#111827] md:text-[30px]">Week 2: build and test privately</h2>
  <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    <li>Connect the agent only to what the access list allows. Prefer read-only access to a copy or test environment.</li>
    <li>Turn on logging before the first test. If you cannot see what the agent read, called and changed, you cannot govern it.</li>
    <li>Test on past cases where you already know the right answer. Include awkward ones: missing information, an angry customer, a request outside scope.</li>
    <li>If the agent connects through MCP, ask the builder which specification version the servers run and how client-supplied state is verified. The OWASP MCP Top 10, still in beta, is a useful question sheet.</li>
    <li>Check what happens when the agent does not know. It should hand over, not guess.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<div class="mb-12" data-reveal>
  <h2 class="mb-5 text-[26px] font-semibold leading-[1.2] tracking-tight text-[#111827] md:text-[30px]">Week 3: run alongside people</h2>
  <p class="mb-5 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    Real work now flows through the agent, but nothing leaves the business without a person’s approval.
  </p>
  <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    <li>The agent drafts; a named reviewer approves, edits or rejects each item.</li>
    <li>Record every edit and rejection with a reason. This is your best evidence about where the agent is weak.</li>
    <li>Track review time per item. Among developers, Stack Overflow’s 2025 survey found 66% pointed to AI output that is “almost right, but not quite”. Expect to see something similar and measure it.</li>
    <li>Fix instructions, data or access based on the log. Change one thing at a time so you know what helped.</li>
    <li>Do not widen scope this week.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<div class="mb-12" data-reveal>
  <h2 class="mb-5 text-[26px] font-semibold leading-[1.2] tracking-tight text-[#111827] md:text-[30px]">Week 4: limited live run and decision</h2>
  <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    <li>Let the agent complete a defined slice of low-risk items without item-by-item approval, only if week 3 met your thresholds. Keep approval for payments, deletions, outbound messages and anything customer-facing.</li>
    <li>Review a sample of results each day, and read the log.</li>
    <li>At the end of the week, compare the numbers with your baseline and write the one-page decision (below).</li>
    <li>Keep the ability to switch the agent off in minutes.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<div class="mb-12" data-reveal>
  <h2 class="mb-5 text-[26px] font-semibold leading-[1.2] tracking-tight text-[#111827] md:text-[30px]">Success measures</h2>
  <p class="mb-5 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    Gartner, as reported by W.Media, said over 40% of agentic AI projects could be cancelled by the end of 2027 because of escalating costs, unclear business value or inadequate risk controls. Our suggestion is to measure those three things directly.
  </p>
  <div class="mb-5 overflow-x-auto"><table class="w-full text-left"><thead><tr><th class="border-b border-gray-200 py-3 pr-4 font-mono text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-[#6B7280]">Area</th><th class="border-b border-gray-200 py-3 pr-4 font-mono text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-[#6B7280]">Measure</th><th class="border-b border-gray-200 py-3 pr-4 font-mono text-[11px] font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-[#6B7280]">How</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-3 pr-4 align-top text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280]">Value</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-3 pr-4 align-top text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280]">Time per item, volume handled, error rate, turnaround</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-3 pr-4 align-top text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280]">Compare with the week 1 baseline</td></tr><tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-3 pr-4 align-top text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280]">Cost</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-3 pr-4 align-top text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280]">Supplier fees, staff review time, setup effort</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-3 pr-4 align-top text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280]">Total for the month, divided by items handled</td></tr><tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-3 pr-4 align-top text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280]">Control</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-3 pr-4 align-top text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280]">Actions outside the access list, incidents, items handed to a person</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-3 pr-4 align-top text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280]">Read from the log, not from the supplier’s summary</td></tr><tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-3 pr-4 align-top text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280]">People</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-3 pr-4 align-top text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280]">Reviewer confidence and staff feedback</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-3 pr-4 align-top text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280]">Short check-in at the end of weeks 3 and 4</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
  <p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    Measure outcomes, not enthusiasm. That advice comes from our post on <a href="/blog/ai-coding-agents-2026-how-developers-actually-work-now/" class="font-medium text-[#5a9100] hover:underline">how developers use coding agents</a>: track review time, defects and delivery speed before and after, not how often the tool is used.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="mb-12" data-reveal>
  <h2 class="mb-5 text-[26px] font-semibold leading-[1.2] tracking-tight text-[#111827] md:text-[30px]">The day-30 decision</h2>
  <ul class="mb-5 list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    <li><strong>Continue:</strong> thresholds met on value, cost and control. Widen scope one step at a time.</li>
    <li><strong>Adjust:</strong> promising but a threshold missed. Name the fix, run another two weeks.</li>
    <li><strong>Stop:</strong> thresholds missed with no clear fix, or the workflow turned out to be better served by plain automation or a booking link. This is a valid, cheap result.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    Write the decision on one page: what you tested, the numbers against baseline, what went wrong, what you would change. If you continue, pick the next workflow with the same five decisions.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="mb-12" data-reveal>
  <h2 class="mb-5 text-[26px] font-semibold leading-[1.2] tracking-tight text-[#111827] md:text-[30px]">Checklist</h2>
  <ul class="mb-5 list-disc space-y-2 pl-6 text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    <li>Workflow chosen, with a written definition of done</li>
    <li>Owner named</li>
    <li>Baseline recorded from real work</li>
    <li>Thresholds for value, cost and control set before week 1 ends</li>
    <li>Access list written; read-only to start</li>
    <li>Logging on before the first test</li>
    <li>Tested on past cases, including awkward ones</li>
    <li>Handover to a person defined and tested</li>
    <li>Reviewer assigned for week 3</li>
    <li>Approval kept for payments, deletions, outbound messages and customer-facing steps</li>
    <li>Switch-off tested</li>
    <li>Day-30 decision written on one page</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">
    Need help running one? We build <a href="/solutions/custom-agent/" class="font-medium text-[#5a9100] hover:underline">custom AI agent development</a>, and you can <a href="/schedule-a-call/" class="font-medium text-[#5a9100] hover:underline">book a call</a>.
  </p>
</div>

