<div class="mb-12 rounded-lg border border-gray-200 bg-gray-50 p-6" data-reveal>
  <h2 class="mb-5 text-[26px] font-semibold leading-[1.2] tracking-tight text-[#111827] md:text-[30px]">Frequently asked questions</h2>
  <div class="mt-6 space-y-6">
    <div><p class="mb-2 text-[17px] font-medium text-[#111827]">How long should an AI agent pilot last?</p><p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">Thirty days is a workable length for one narrow workflow: enough time to set a baseline, test with a person reviewing, run a limited live period and decide. A broader scope needs longer. The aim is a decision, not a launch.</p></div>
    <div><p class="mb-2 text-[17px] font-medium text-[#111827]">What should the pilot measure?</p><p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">Gartner, as reported by W.Media, links cancelled agentic projects to escalating costs, unclear business value and inadequate risk controls. Our suggestion is to measure those three things: cost, value against your own baseline, and control (access, logs and incidents).</p></div>
    <div><p class="mb-2 text-[17px] font-medium text-[#111827]">Who needs to be involved?</p><p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">A named owner who is accountable for the result, a reviewer who checks the agent's work, and a supplier or builder who can answer questions about access, logging and failure. Staff whose work changes should hear about it in week one.</p></div>
    <div><p class="mb-2 text-[17px] font-medium text-[#111827]">What if the pilot fails?</p><p class="text-[16px] leading-relaxed text-[#6B7280] md:text-[17px]">That is a useful result. A pilot that ends with a clear reason to stop, such as unclear outcomes or too much review time, costs far less than a full rollout that gets cancelled. Write down what you learned and choose a different workflow or wait.</p></div>
  </div>
</div>

